SUMMARY:Day 10 Evening Ledger
DESCRIPTION:Tonight's question: Write that boy a short letter. What did he
  need to hear that he never heard?\n\nTHE LEDGER (10 min)\n1. Lies told to
 day (#): what\, to whom\n2. Lies caught before I spoke (#)\n3. Corrections
  made (#)\n4. The trigger: who\, where\, how tired I was\, and the fear (f
 ailure / rejection / conflict / looking weak)\n5. The story in my head: "I
 f I tell the truth\, ___ will happen."\n6. What actually happened when I t
 old the truth\n7. Tomorrow's risk moment\, written as an if-then: "If ___\
 , then I'll say ___."\n8. One line of prayer or thanks
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Day 10 Evening Ledger
TRIGGER:PT0M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:truth-ledger-31@bethemansystem.com
DTSTAMP:20261125T142333Z
DTSTART:20261206T063000
DTEND:20261206T063500
SUMMARY:Day 11 Morning: Shame vs. guilt
DESCRIPTION:Week 2: The Fear Underneath. Every lie is protecting something
 .\n\nGuilt says "I did something wrong." Shame says "I am wrong." Shame dr
 ives lies. Catch shame talk in your head today and write it down.\n\nHOW T
 O DO IT\n1. Keep a list today of every harsh thing your mind says about yo
 u: "I'm a liar\," "I'm a failure\," "I'm worthless."\n2. Tonight\, pick th
 e three loudest.\n3. Rewrite each in this exact form: "I did ___. I can ma
 ke it right by ___."\n\nBefore you go\, write today's if-then: "If ___ hap
 pens\, then I'll say ___."\n\nWhy it works: Shame hides. Guilt repairs. Da
 y 11 teaches you to turn shame sentences into guilt sentences. You can rep
 air something you did. You can't repair who you are. (Tangney & Dearing\, 
 Shame and Guilt (2002) and later reviews)
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Day 11 Morning: Shame vs. guilt
TRIGGER:PT0M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
